I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, diffraction pattern, and the supplied refinement helpers first. Then I’ll screen phase candidates, audit any structural models, refine competing complete-pattern models in GSAS-II, and verify all exports against the final project.

The sample is a low-temperature BaCO₃/WO₃ reaction measured with Cu Kα radiation over roughly 10° onward, with no stated product or phase count. I’m now mapping the available databases and GSAS-II environment, then I’ll extract peak positions before making any chemistry-based assignments.

Initial screening strongly supports orthorhombic witherite BaCO₃ and monoclinic WO₃: each independently explains a distinct set of intense peaks, including the four-peak cluster near 23–25° and repeated matches through high angle. BaWO₄ is not yet justified; its strongest nominal line overlaps a WO₃ line, so I’ll test it only after refining the two-phase baseline and examining unique residuals.

The modern BaCO₃ model passes the structural audit. For WO₃, one superficially good database match was rejected because its atom list creates implausibly short O···O contacts; I’m carrying three physically valid WO₃ polymorph/setting alternatives through the same staged refinement instead. The first branch is converging without dropped variables, though its unit cell/zero-offset stage is deliberately being allowed extra cycles before comparison.

The first complete two-phase model reaches Rwp ≈ 8.25% with physically sensible cells and roughly 54/46 wt% BaCO₃/WO₃. The orthorhombic WO₃ alternative is clearly inferior (≈13.39% after cell refinement). The lower-symmetry triclinic WO₃ alternative is genuinely competitive only after its cell is allowed to move, so I’m retaining it through full convergence rather than selecting on an early-stage score.

The angular-correction test rejects the joint zero/displacement model as >99.9% correlated. A specimen-displacement-only correction is modestly but consistently better than zero-only for the monoclinic model (Rwp 7.735% vs 7.780%). I’m waiting for the same matched test on the triclinic contender before comparing the two structures again from their updated states.

The envelope tests favor a refined polarization factor (0.781, physically admissible) over surface roughness; the roughness branch drove one coefficient negative and made the pair essentially perfectly correlated, so it is rejected. Angle-dependent Gaussian and Lorentzian broadening terms each give smaller but real improvements, and I’m retesting them from the selected polarization state to avoid keeping redundant terms.

The retained global profile is now the smaller interpretable set: refined W/X/Y broadening plus polarization and specimen displacement. Adding another Gaussian term became negative and gave no meaningful gain; adding the alternative Gaussian term was unstable. I’m now screening crystallite size and microstrain independently for each phase, as required, before considering texture.

The leading microstructure explanation is a finite WO₃ crystallite size. I’m completing the matched BaCO₃ test, then I’ll retest the remaining profile terms from that updated baseline and challenge the triclinic WO₃ assignment against the monoclinic alternative.

The BaCO₃ size term is singular with the global Lorentzian width, and both strain branches become unstable or nonphysical when paired with WO₃ size, so I’m rejecting them. I’m now screening simple one-axis preferred-orientation terms phase-by-phase; only a stable, whole-pattern improvement will be retained.

Both retained texture corrections are small but statistically resolved and improve several separated angle regions: BaCO₃ [001] gives the main gain, while WO₃ [010] adds a smaller four-region improvement. I’m carrying that minimal pair forward and now repeating the microstructure tests and the monoclinic-vs-triclinic structure comparison under the same nuisance model.

With identical refined nuisance terms, monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c) now fits slightly better than triclinic P-1 despite using fewer cell degrees of freedom; it is also the expected room-temperature WO₃ symmetry. The distinction is genuinely close, so I’m treating it as a model-selection uncertainty and repeating every optional profile/microstructure test on the monoclinic baseline before freezing it.

The monoclinic rerun confirms the same simplifications: U and V become nonphysical/unstable, surface roughness is negative and 99.6% correlated, BaCO₃ size is singular, and the apparent strain gain is an exact phase-swapping degeneracy. Those terms are excluded. I’m now rerunning the zero/displacement branch selector on the mature model, then I’ll perform leave-one-out and retained-term removal checks.

The retained terms all pass matched removal tests: removing polarization, Y, WO₃ size, BaCO₃ texture, or WO₃ texture worsens Rwp by 0.05–0.48 percentage points. Dropping either phase worsens Rwp to roughly 25–27%, confirming both inventories independently. The final model remains monoclinic WO₃, but only narrowly over the triclinic alternative; I’ll preserve that caveat in the report.

The exported CIFs and final project pass the structural/contact and replay audits. Before writing the report, I’m doing one last chemistry-specific control: testing a clean scheelite BaWO₄ model, because the first BaWO₄ database record was malformed and the 400 °C precursor context makes this the most important possible third phase to exclude explicitly.

The BaWO₄ control refined to an apparent 0.29 wt% but failed the required structural test: its strongest unique lines at 28.33° and 32.15° make the local fit worse, while the numerical gain comes mainly from overlaps. I have therefore left it out and explicitly marked its trace fraction as indeterminate. The final two-phase files are complete; I’m running the final schema/readback audit now.

Identified crystalline composition:

- BaCO₃ (witherite, Pnma/Pmcn): 52.36 wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic P2₁/c): 47.64 wt%

Rwp = 6.980%, Rp = 5.293%, GOF = 2.007. Practical fraction uncertainty is about ±0.6 wt%. Triclinic WO₃ is near-tied; trace BaWO₄ could not be determined reliably and was excluded.

